(Q3: What was the immediate cause of the First World War?) Tj
/F1 9 Tf
0 -13 Td
(Ans: On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, the heir to the throne of Austria-Hungary, was assassinated by Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb, at Sarajevo. [2 Marks]) Tj
0 -13 Td
0 -8 Td
/F1 9.5 Tf
(Q4: Distinguish between Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats.) Tj
/F1 9 Tf
0 -13 Td
(Ans: 1\) Western Ghats: Continuous range, higher altitude \(avg 2,000m to 3,000m\), origin of major rivers like Godavari, Krishna, Cauvery. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -13 Td
(     2\) Eastern Ghats: Discontinuous range, lower elevation \(avg 1,100m\), dissected by eastward-flowing rivers entering the Bay of Bengal. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
0 -8 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(### 5 Marks Detailed Answer \(Sura Guide Exam Standard\)) Tj
/F1 9 Tf
0 -15 Td
/F1 9.5 Tf
(Q5: Describe the major physical divisions of Northern Mountains \(Himalayas\).) Tj
/F1 9 Tf
0 -13 Td
(Ans: Outline: Introduction - Trans-Himalayas - Greater Himalayas \(Himadri\) - Lesser Himalayas \(Himachal\) - Outer Himalayas \(Siwaliks\).) Tj
0 -13 Td
(Step 1: Introduction: The Himalayas act as the northern crown of India extending 2,400 km from Indus to Brahmaputra. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
(Step 2: Trans-Himalayas \(Tibetan Himalayas\): Lies north of the Great Himalayas; includes Karakoram, Ladakh, and Zaskar ranges. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
(Step 3: Greater Himalayas \(Himadri\): Most continuous range with average height of 6,000 m; includes Mt. Everest \(8,848 m\) and Kanchenjunga. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
(Step 4: Lesser Himalayas \(Himachal\): Altitude 3,700m - 4,500m; famous for hill stations like Shimla, Mussoorie, Nainital, Darjeeling. [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
(Step 5: Outer Himalayas \(Siwaliks\): Youngest range with altitude 900m - 1,100m; longitudinal valleys between Himachal and Siwalik are called Duns \(e.g. Dehradun\). [1 Mark]) Tj
0 -12 Td
